PING(8)                        P32W Unix                         PING(8)

NAME
       ping - is a host there?

SYNOPSIS
       ping [-c count] [-s size] host

DESCRIPTION
       ping sends ICMP echo requests to host, one a second, and prints
       each answer with the time it took. It stops after count
       requests, or at ^C, and prints how many were answered and the
       shortest, average and longest times.

       Each request is built by ping itself: ICMP type 8, its
       checksum, ping's process id and a sequence number, then size
       bytes of data (56 by default, 1000 at most). It goes through
       an ICMP socket (socket(AF_INET, SOCK_DGRAM, IPPROTO_ICMP)); the
       network coprocessor puts it in an IP packet.

EXAMPLES
       ping -c 3 10.0.2.2

SEE ALSO
       ifconfig(8), socket(2)

BUGS
       Times are counted in clock ticks, so they read 0, 20, 40 ms and
       so on. The IP header of an answer (its time to live) is not
       seen. The exit status is 0 if any answer came, else 1.
